<interrogationes unitas="dispositio">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I lay out a widget tree (header, growing body, footer) without hand pixel arithmetic?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_creare dispositio_formam_initiare dispositio_addere dispositio_computare dispositio_fines" via="lib/kalendarium.c">Create
      a Dispositio from a piscina, fill a DispositioForma per node
      (start from dispositio_formam_initiare, then set directio, the
      latitudo and altitudo DispositioMensura, spacing, gap and
      alignment), add it with dispositio_addere under its parent's
      index (-1 for the root), call dispositio_computare with the
      surface size in cells, then read each node's rectangle with
      dispositio_fines(d, index). It is the Clay model: fit, grow,
      fixed and percent sizes per axis.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what units does dispositio work in, and how do I get pixel rectangles for a window?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_ad_pixela dispositio_fines Fines">Everything
      is integer CELLS, so every edge lands on a cell boundary by
      construction. dispositio_ad_pixela(fines, columnae, lineae,
      latitudo, altitudo, cw, ch) multiplies by the cell size, and a
      node that reaches the right or bottom edge of the cell grid is
      stretched to the surface's real pixel edge, since a window is
      usually not an exact multiple of the cell size.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the implicit root, and what does parent -1 mean?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_addere dispositio_computare">As
      in Clay there is a hidden root: a row (DISPOSITIO_LINEA) fixed
      at the surface size given to dispositio_computare, with no
      padding and no gap. Nodes added with parent -1 are its
      children, laid out left to right. Usually you add a single
      top-level node there with CRESCENS on both axes and build under
      it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make a child take the remaining space, or a fixed or percentage size?
    <responsum symbolum="DispositioMensura DispositioGenus">Set
      the axis DispositioMensura: DISPOSITIO_CRESCENS grows into what is
      left (bounded by minimum and maximum, 0 meaning no maximum),
      DISPOSITIO_FIXA takes valor cells, DISPOSITIO_PARS takes valor
      hundredths of the parent's inner size (after padding and, along
      the parent's direction, gaps; rounded down), and DISPOSITIO_APTA fits the content. minimum and
      maximum apply to APTA and CRESCENS only. Several growing
      children share the space Clay-style: the smallest grow first,
      and an uneven remainder is spread across them, not given to the
      first.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a node hold text and size itself to fit it?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_computare DispositioMensor DispositioForma">Yes,
      one line of it: set forma.textus and pass a DispositioMensor to
      dispositio_computare that returns the width of a chorda in cells
      (wide runes may count II in a terminal). The text becomes a
      virtual first child one line high, and its minimum is the widest
      space-separated word. With a NIHIL mensor the text is ignored.
      Newlines and wrapping are not supported yet.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I free the text I put in forma.textus right after dispositio_addere?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_addere dispositio_computare">No.
      dispositio_addere copies the DispositioForma, but the chorda in
      textus is copied by value, so it still points at your bytes, and
      the mensor reads them during dispositio_computare. Keep the text
      alive until computare returns.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I re-run layout every frame without the piscina growing?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_vacare dispositio_computare">dispositio_vacare
      removes all nodes and keeps the memory, so the next frame's
      dispositio_addere calls reuse it; indices restart at 0. Calling
      dispositio_computare again on the same tree with a new surface
      size simply recomputes every rectangle.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I know dispositio_addere failed, and what does dispositio_fines give for a bad index?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_addere dispositio_fines dispositio_numerus">dispositio_addere
      returns -1 when d or forma is NIHIL, when parens is below -1, or
      when the parent index does not exist yet (a parent must be added
      before its children); otherwise
      it returns the node's index in order of addition.
      dispositio_fines for an unknown index returns an all-zero Fines
      rather than failing, so check the index first (dispositio_numerus
      gives the count).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what happens when children do not fit in their parent?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_computare DispositioForma">Along
      the parent's direction, APTA and CRESCENS children shrink
      (largest first) down to their content minimum; FIXA and PARS
      never shrink. If the parent has praecidere set on that axis
      nothing is compressed and the children simply overflow. Along
      the main axis an overflowing row is placed from the start even
      when alignment is MEDIUM or FINIS; across it, a too-big child
      centred with MEDIUM gets a negative offset (rounded down, as
      -3/2 gives -2).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are dispositio results not identical to Clay's?
    <responsum symbolum="dispositio_computare" via="include/dispositio.h tools/dispositio_oraculum.sh">Clay
      uses floats; dispositio is integer and names its divergences in
      the header: centring rounds down (even when negative), percent
      sizes round down, and when grow or shrink space does not divide
      evenly each edge is the floor of the exact edge, so the remainder
      is spread instead of going to the first children. Everything
      else is meant to match, and tools/dispositio_oraculum.sh runs
      the real Clay (a ../clay clone pinned at e6cc369; exit 2 if it is
      missing) on the same STML tree as the oracle.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
